// ===========================================================================
// 03 - RATES AND NORMALISATION
// ===========================================================================
// Volume answers "where are the most cases". Rate answers "where is a woman
// most likely to be affected". They disagree, and the disagreement is the most
// interesting finding in the project:
//
//   West Bengal   - 1st on volume (302,143 cases), 6th on intensity
//   Assam         - highest 2021 intensity at 84.8 per lakh women, ~1.9x WB
//
// A dashboard that only ranks by volume is a population map with extra steps.
// ===========================================================================


// --- Denominator -----------------------------------------------------------
// SUMX over VALUES, not SUM. Female Population 2011 lives on the state
// dimension at one row per state; a plain SUM would be correct here too, but
// SUMX over VALUES(State) makes the grain explicit and stays correct if the
// dimension ever gains a second row per state (a boundary-change version, say).
Female Population =
SUMX (
    VALUES ( DimState[State] ),
    CALCULATE ( MAX ( DimState[Female Population 2011] ) )
)

Population =
SUMX (
    VALUES ( DimState[State] ),
    CALCULATE ( MAX ( DimState[Population 2011] ) )
)


// --- Point-in-time rate ----------------------------------------------------
// Cases per 100,000 women. "Per lakh" is the Indian convention and matches how
// NCRB publishes its own rate tables, so the number is directly comparable to
// the published figures.
//
// Use this with a single year in context. With several years selected the
// numerator accumulates while the denominator stays fixed, which makes the
// number grow with the width of the window - see the annualised version below.
DV Rate per Lakh Women =
DIVIDE ( [DV Cases], [Female Population] ) * 100000


// --- Window-safe rate ------------------------------------------------------
// Divides by the number of years actually in context, so the value is an
// average annual rate and stays interpretable at any slicer width. This is the
// one to put on a card; the point-in-time version belongs on a per-year axis.
DV Rate per Lakh Women (Annual Avg) =
VAR YearsInContext = DISTINCTCOUNT ( FactCrimes[year] )
RETURN
    DIVIDE ( DIVIDE ( [DV Cases], YearsInContext ), [Female Population] ) * 100000


// --- All-crime rate --------------------------------------------------------
All Crime Rate per Lakh Women =
DIVIDE ( [All Crime Cases], [Female Population] ) * 100000


// --- Rate vs the national benchmark ----------------------------------------
// Expresses a state as a multiple of the all-India rate for the same years.
// 1.0 is the national average; 2.0 means twice the national intensity. Easier
// to read on a map legend than an absolute rate, and it survives a year change.
DV Rate vs National =
VAR StateRate = [DV Rate per Lakh Women]
VAR NationalRate =
    CALCULATE (
        DIVIDE ( [DV Cases], [Female Population] ) * 100000,
        REMOVEFILTERS ( DimState )
    )
RETURN
    DIVIDE ( StateRate, NationalRate )


// --- Caveat surfaced as a measure ------------------------------------------
// Rates use a fixed Census 2011 denominator, because it is the only
// census-grade population figure available anywhere in a 2001-2021 window
// (the 2021 census was postponed). That makes every rate here a comparable
// index across states, not a live incidence rate - a state whose population
// grew fastest since 2011 has its rate overstated at the end of the series.
//
// Bound to a tooltip on every rate visual so the caveat travels with the number.
Rate Basis Note =
"Cases per 100,000 women. Denominator: Census 2011 (fixed). "
    & "Comparable across states; not a live incidence rate."
